      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Stories from the field",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-stories-from-the-field.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "the field\nThe Global Influenza Surveillance & Response \nSystem at 70: a public health resource for the world \nGoing from strength to strength in the fight against influenza and other respiratory diseases.  \n\n\"Influenza is not a national issue; it’s not a national \nphenomenon – it’s a global phenomenon and you can’t \nwork on it isolated in the country.\"\n- Dr Olav Hungnes, Director of National Influenza Centre, \nNorwegian Institute of Public Health\n\nThe birth of GISRS\nIn 1952, the WHO Expert Committee on Influenza met \nand created a global network for influenza surveillance \nconsisting of WHO’s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents stories from the field, beginning with the 70th anniversary of the Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System, which grew from a 1952 network into one spanning 127 countries, areas and territories. It describes the scale of testing by national influenza centres during the COVID-19 years and GISRS's role in providing viruses for vaccines. Country examples include Nepal's influenza centre taking on SARS-CoV-2 testing and the expansion of integrated surveillance under GISRS Plus. Other stories cover planning to monitor a future pandemic response and integrated respiratory surveillance in Timor-Leste.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
